course: dictionary
lesson: 2775
title: Strong feelings in words
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words tell you how strongly someone feels. When you meet a new word, look at the words around it for clues. The Rat read some invitations and said they were «simply disgraceful». Then he «confiscated the lot», which means he took them all away. Someone who takes things away is very cross, so disgraceful must mean very bad.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. Does «The Rat said the invitations were very bad and shameful» still make sense? Yes, so you have the right meaning. Ask yourself: does the speaker sound cross or shocked? If so, the word is probably showing strong feeling.
explain: Adults often use this word in real life. They say «a disgraceful way to behave», «absolutely disgraceful», or «it's disgraceful that…». The word is stronger than a small mistake. It tells the reader that the speaker thinks something is very wrong.
fact: Disgraceful means very bad, shameful, and deserving strong disapproval.
fact: «Disgraceful» can also describe something shocking that makes people feel ashamed for whoever did it.
fact: Common phrases include «absolutely disgraceful» and «it's disgraceful that…».
fact: Using «disgraceful» shows that the speaker is very cross and shocked, not just a little unhappy.
exercise: «It was disgraceful to cheat in the spelling test.» What does disgraceful mean here? | Very tired and sleepy | Very quick and clever | Very bright and shiny | *Very bad and shameful
exercise: Mum says, «It's disgraceful that the bins were left full all week.» What is she showing? | She is delighted and proud | *She is very cross and shocked | She is hungry | She is bored and sleepy
exercise: Which phrase is a good example of «disgraceful» in use? | a cold glass of milk | a bright and sunny day | *a disgraceful way to behave | a soft, warm blanket
test: The Rat said the invitations were «simply disgraceful», so he took them all away. Why did he do that? | He had lost them | He was pleased and wanted to keep them | He wanted to send them to his friends | *He was very cross because they were shameful
test: Which sentence uses disgraceful correctly? | The disgraceful sun shone brightly on the hill. | The puppy's disgraceful little tail wagged all day. | We ate a disgraceful picnic in the park. | *The referee said the team's cheating was disgraceful.
exam: Which word means nearly the same as disgraceful? | gentle | tidy | *shameful | cheerful
